!ReelEGL - Reel, drawing through EGL (an example)       0.1.21
===================================================================

ReelEGL is the Reel video player built a second way: the picture goes
through riscos-mesa's EGL instead of Reel's own sprite plotting. It is
here as a working example of an EGL program (riscos-ffmpeg's ffegl
library, ffegl_draw_surface, on the reelcore player core that Reel uses
too), and to compare the two. Everything else - the
controls, keys, menus, sound, web addresses and yt-dlp's output - is the
same as Reel: see !Reel's !Help.
Both can run at once (they have different icon bar icons: ReelEGL's
reel has a green hub).

How it draws
------------
- In the window, the picture is an EGL "work area surface": a surface
  of fixed size at the top of the window's work area, under which the
  controls are ordinary Wimp icons. Each frame is converted by swscale
  straight into the surface (EGL_KHR_lock_surface) and eglSwapBuffers
  shows it; redraws plot it with eglPlotSurfaceRISCOS.
- Full screen uses EGL's whole-screen surface. With Vsync (full screen)
  ticked (the window menu; the default) EGL plots each picture after
  waiting for the screen's refresh (eglSwapInterval 1): no tearing.
  Untick it and frames go straight into screen memory
  (EGL_SINGLE_BUFFER): no plot at all, the fastest way, but you may see
  tearing. *Set ReelEGL$NoVsync 1 starts with it off.
- Hardware acceleration (the window menu, as in Reel): with the
  VideoOverlay module, pictures go straight into a hardware overlay (as
  YV12, the decoder's own format) and the display scales them, instead
  of being converted into the EGL surface. The surface is then drawn only
  while the overlay is hidden (something over the picture, paused, at
  the end). ReelEGL talks to VideoOverlay itself: riscos-mesa's EGL
  overlays (EGL_RISCOS_overlay) cover visible-area RGB surfaces 1:1, and
  ReelEGL's picture is a work area surface.
  A picture that comes before the overlay's last change of picture (at
  the screen's refresh) waits for it without holding up decoding;
  Media info's Drawing line counts those ("waited") and any a newer one
  replaced before it was shown ("replaced").
- Subtitles (V, J, the Subtitles menu, subtitle files beside the video
  or dropped on it), chapters (Page Up/Down, the Chapters menu), frame
  steps (. and , while paused) and turned phone videos work as in Reel
  (see its !Help); subtitles are drawn into the picture.
- S (or Stats on the picture on the window menu) draws YouTube-style
  "Stats for nerds" figures into the picture, as in Reel (see its !Help):
  into the picture itself, so they show over a hardware overlay too.
- Media info opens beside the player (or above or below it) where there's
  room, so it doesn't cover the picture.
- The mini player (M) is a second window; its picture is a work area
  surface of its own, made when it opens (only one surface exists at a
  time, so switching makes the other window's surface again).

Needs
-----
As Reel. The EGL and OpenGL libraries are built in (from riscos-mesa
20.3.5-7pre12); no modules are needed for them.

